#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125604} :activity/id #uuid "c46f3c61-cb8f-5de3-a323-513dfb71dceb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048942 :topic/id #uuid "cdb710e4-10cf-5554-8041-f24c448bbdae" :topic/math-academy-id 1930} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125604 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125863 :tutorial/content "A **neighborhood** of a point $x_0\\in \\mathbb R$ is the set of points that are \"close to\" $x_0.$ \n\nMore precisely, let's define a number $\\delta >0$, where $\\delta$ is usually very small.  A **neighborhood** of a point $x_0$ is the set of all points whose distance from $x_0$ is smaller than $\\delta.$ \n\nIn one-dimensional space, a neighborhood of a point $x_0\\in\\mathbb R$ is an open interval $\\left(x_0-\\delta, x_0+\\delta\\right),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/04/04016c37be4c907eabc144db16eb757dff773907262ccd04941cf27ddd2e49d6.png)\n\nFor example, if we set $\\delta = 0.1,$ then the corresponding neighborhood of the point $x_0 = 2$ is as follows:\n\n![](images/03/0329bef56b8b903eb13510b7344fd7ca488d34c8d0c85c7648cebc8ecf7cfa40.png)\n\nNote that $x=1.9$ and $x=2.1$ are *not* included in this neighborhood since their distance from the point $x_0$ is *not* smaller than $\\delta.$ \n\nWe can represent a neighborhood of $x_0$ using set notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x : | x- x_0| < \\delta \\}\n$$\n\nWe often refer to sets of this type as *open* neighborhoods since they contain all points whose distance from $x_0$ is *smaller than* (but not equal) to $\\delta.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b2ea7b59-5287-5d12-b53f-32a9254b8ba7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6818 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "44c26f5a-57b1-5e3b-b126-e2f92e8f7bac" :step/math-academy-id 41386 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125622}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124191 :question/id #uuid "da9684b4-df8d-531d-a200-cf54685067c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-10913"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417818}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "13578b7b-3533-5f2d-b6bc-c8214f0f163b" :step/math-academy-id 41387 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125615}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124192 :question/id #uuid "7e78e3a1-836a-53af-95a3-0a9d192cc7d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-10924"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050229}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435511}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "b2201caf-3e1b-53b4-b391-8a27cfbbdce1" :step/math-academy-id 41433 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125607}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125607 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125864 :tutorial/content "In two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of a point $\\mathbf x_0\\in \\mathbb R^2$ is a disc of radius $\\delta$ centered at $\\mathbf x_0$ that does not include the bounding circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nThe definitions of the interior and boundary points are the same as for the one-dimensional case. The only difference is that now a neighborhood of a point is a disc.\n\nFor example, consider the set $S$ and points $A,$ $B, C,$ and $D,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7e037fd998c235ff7b721e7187d44814700ac5f41a256920f34f248c798c520.png)\n\nLet's classify each of these as interior or boundary points (or neither):\n\n- $A$ is an interior point since *there exists* a neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set.\n\n- $B$ is neither an interior nor a boundary point because *there exists* a neighborhood of $B$ that does not contain points from the set.\n\n- $C$ is a boundary point since *every* neighborhood of $C$ contains points that lie in the set and points that do not lie in the set.\n\n- $D$ is a boundary point since *every* neighborhood of $D$ contains points that lie in the set and points that do not lie in the set.\n\nNotice that although $C$ and $D$ are both boundary points, $C\\notin S$ and yet $D\\in S.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bfda97a2-2fe7-5c9a-a067-dbf903ba2111" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6825 :tutorial/title "Interior and Boundary Points of Sets in the Plane"} :step/id #uuid "fd03cb93-6f1c-502a-9888-3a2700a9c8a7" :step/math-academy-id 41436 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125605}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125615 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124199 :question/id #uuid "1f21955b-70dc-5b61-8b19-42860de1be4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-11031"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417817}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "23fe8ae7-b1b8-5490-93e8-49b20cd742f0" :step/math-academy-id 41912 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125616}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124642 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124200 :question/id #uuid "9b01032c-d4e6-5660-a3c9-477e67c011a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-11032"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049699}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478455}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"} :step/id #uuid "dc8051c9-6e2f-5ded-82a6-e902adddd2c5" :step/math-academy-id 41916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125870 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose that $S$ is a set of real numbers. A point $x_0$ is called an **interior point** of $S$ if *some* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ is *fully contained* inside $S.$ \n\nFor example, consider the set $S=[0,1)$ and the points $A$ and $B,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a1/a12c60b6f60ca7c35162fb1ddf0d0d507fe80e73e621c451c21f0b882034ae21.png)\n\nThe point $A$ is an interior point of $S,$ but $B$ is not:\n\n- The point $A$ is an interior point of $S$ since there exists a neighborhood of $A$ that is *fully contained* inside $S.$\n\n- The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $S$ since there is no neighborhood of $B$ that is *fully contained* inside $S.$ In other words, any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nNote that neither $x=0$ nor $x=1$ are interior points. To see why, let's draw a neighborhood of each point:\n\n![](images/a9/a923732241443a0fd6a5f53dab7de2d346f5704a0b034c276e64dc3df2746f4f.png)\n\n \n\nFrom here, we can deduce the following:\n\n- The point $x=0$ is *not* an interior point of $S$ since there is no neighborhood of $x=0$ that is *fully* contained inside $S.$\n\n- The point $x=1$ is also *not* an interior point of $S$ since there is no neighborhood of $x=1$ that is *fully* contained inside $S.$\n\nThe set containing all interior points of a set $S$ is called the **interior** of $S,$ and is denoted $\\textrm{int}\\,S.$ \n\nFor the set $[0,1),$ its interior is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{int}\\,S = (0,1).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "da6d3c21-fe0a-566e-a6e0-117f10988923" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7685 :tutorial/title "Interior Points"} :step/id #uuid "99fecfa4-9502-52e4-aff9-d84b074446bb" :step/math-academy-id 44624 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125623}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125623 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125871 :tutorial/content "A point $x_0$ is a **boundary point** of $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $x_0$ contains points that *are* in $S$ *and* points that are *not* in $S.$ \n\nLet's consider the set $S=[0,1),$ the points $x=0, x=1,$ and neighborhoods of these points.\n\n![](images/a9/a923732241443a0fd6a5f53dab7de2d346f5704a0b034c276e64dc3df2746f4f.png)\n\nBoth $x=0$ and $x=1$ are boundary points of $S{:}$\n\n- The point $x=0$ is a boundary point because *every* open neighborhood contains points that belong to $S$ and points that do not belong to $S.$\n\n- The point $x=1$ is a boundary point because *every* open neighborhood contains points that belong to $S$ and points that do not belong to $S.$\n\nThe set of all boundary points of a set $S$ is called the **boundary** of $S,$ and is denoted as $\\partial S.$\n\nFor the set $[0,1)$ above, its boundary contains two points only, namely $x=0$ and $x=1,$ and we write\n\n$$\n\\partial S = \\{0,1\\}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "54b0ce40-c312-54e6-87c5-ba2b69af3dae" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7686 :tutorial/title "Boundary Points"} :step/id #uuid "2b346783-d47e-5992-9033-dcf4627c7e39" :step/math-academy-id 44625 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125606}}] :activity/title "Interior and Boundary Points" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124723}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417821} :activity/id #uuid "1525b4dc-2341-50b4-9999-2ad38292c9ae" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048942 :topic/id #uuid "cdb710e4-10cf-5554-8041-f24c448bbdae" :topic/math-academy-id 1930} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417819 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f955c119-f900-5747-9855-7eb87295f2f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473260} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc29abc-f9e8-53f8-a218-d470e670dac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473261}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473261} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0b57ac4-5833-5f98-85f6-8d01d8cfbdee" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474025} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da87805c-dd59-5cd4-a00e-ea9010aca456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473262} {:answer/id #uuid "ce41ce68-62e4-5bd8-8ba7-9e969d0aef89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473263}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473262} :answer-field/id #uuid "99f96f24-dff3-5ad2-ae8b-117ac4e4cad2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474026} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8eb19004-1309-5d14-8763-0cdb11e1bbb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473264} {:answer/id #uuid "64416903-13ab-5932-b3c4-6697b70d4774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473265}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186473264} :answer-field/id #uuid "85b6a004-2b46-516a-adc3-ac69774aa23d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4db015a0-682b-5ff4-909b-ad19f05ab168" :question/math-academy-id "q-71826" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b838b945d0b4002d8ae2238af5992c83dcf23b2985c448c716a5caf8de31b369.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/be/be1ece00243777f3f6b6973319e15ea1e5c8556b2452b61a451713f9dcb86595.png)\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only'."} :step/id #uuid "05f3aec1-db2e-5c3d-b48a-0200d46f45e8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417822}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417820 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417818 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c50983-8391-5870-8348-d7f660e46076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472748} {:answer/id #uuid "60e11f58-a5dd-5c6e-b89c-b922fde1c5fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472749}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472749} :answer-field/id #uuid "143a9e68-361d-54b4-9f3d-e48cc583f56a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473769} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6913eb54-9ddc-554e-82da-aa99b31eea06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472750} {:answer/id #uuid "03c27322-4cea-5897-a805-a6c4a07342da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472751}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472751} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c72ab19-8f7b-5385-a77a-7bded7712b10" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473770} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9f94601-7133-564b-ac38-53504ef01422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472752} {:answer/id #uuid "b58541fc-27f2-5130-a4c2-74d24b8dffa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472753}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472753} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d9085bd-5d71-5a86-abfb-326a5e169143" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f7105850-e717-5535-8a28-db3aaf9f3031" :question/math-academy-id "q-159495" :question/problem "![](images/19/19174ec33e43c900773031043d5de200e892f5e3ee51704d0800b6a944147155.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/43/43362800e91cbb57c22fc6851c10f00927a030a790dea9284e69a5c7c3d4ae25.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III'."} :step/id #uuid "09199b9d-3f40-5adc-9fbb-571a33f5f584"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417821 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23591f63-c86d-5f91-9a17-a690666923d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448578} {:answer/id #uuid "be31c6af-3a33-55f9-8fe1-63efb93650a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448579}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448579} :answer-field/id #uuid "26a890ed-051b-51e6-b54d-7c194bdaf7f9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449329} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e844f5b-4f06-5bf8-946e-b743a531b0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448580} {:answer/id #uuid "1847ce53-72b0-5d27-b50b-d4102ef44f22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448581}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448581} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c644840-d5ba-52dd-ae49-d42747e8e8ca" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449330} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68a62ca0-3bd2-53c6-b360-1371ecc0ba04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448582} {:answer/id #uuid "587abaee-0245-5ac8-bcc3-421b0ac4056a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448583}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448583} :answer-field/id #uuid "78ebfedd-9fc3-5873-908e-1133f982c35c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5b10043b-af03-5e4b-8ef7-b7ee2892ed6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36409" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- **II.** The point $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ belongs to $A$\n- **III.** The point $Q(-1,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or inside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/bb/bbc3e1bfb40c12cfdc655b4207c5f34ea0a01ce254b3f04b96b05ffbbad3f5af.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ and $Q(-1,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/10/100a2b2c1aa58cdfa59980cae4253e1de8b4b2c984c0793c66f7e7cc6edb5211.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is fully contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'"} :step/id #uuid "15c853c6-73a1-5b09-aa32-230241fa286d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417819}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417822 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417817 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca900fdb-2d62-5862-8f58-5e2c93efbb46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453436} {:answer/id #uuid "839c8d82-74a4-5464-b707-309afd311002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453437}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453437} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e5b6f56-ac9e-5312-8788-c82ee0344df5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454468} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eb2be68-e58e-5cf5-a802-398a76d09aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453438} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1f7b94-44bb-5eab-8fad-f6e5da807f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453439}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453439} :answer-field/id #uuid "49d1f656-b70c-5cbc-b720-c740ebcddde4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454469} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66605df2-8aaf-504d-949d-2dc2c5eb81cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453440} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb9751c-c1ad-5871-aa80-62619467576e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453441}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453441} :answer-field/id #uuid "54080e51-b892-57e7-b433-ee3c85f02bbe" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dbe53a96-4c4c-5897-ba48-d4861ec6cb49" :question/math-academy-id "q-158547" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y>2x-3},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=2x-3}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(1,-1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- **II.** The point $Q(0,0)$ belongs to $A$\n- **III.** The point $Q(0,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie above the line $y=2x-3.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/5a/5af40a400e5ea4942a1cf89b09184a277df96d252e4a7fee41bd2df7024a631f.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,-1)$ and $Q(0,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/27/27b978864eac65c93c7886b2d54dfede7130f0f93a16ef96421cd0e5741a6940.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1,-1)$ satisfy $y=2x-3.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the coordinates of $Q(0,0)$ satisfy the inequality $y>2x-3.$ Hence, $Q$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is entirely contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'"} :step/id #uuid "209dd426-c134-5aa2-9527-59de41410725" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417823}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417823 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417816 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fb12b54-0323-5408-9f63-bd359e7b2e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453448} {:answer/id #uuid "164d2254-207a-594f-a70d-382d51b3f8e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453449}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453449} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5032cdf-c59e-5e33-880d-955f6d02841e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454474} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3427dac4-97f1-5765-aafc-94068cf9677e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453450} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0ad7a7-e7bc-5678-aca2-5d4474e68307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453451}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453450} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3387072-c1bc-59ee-8d45-dc6cd0e5411d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454475} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42ad1ee2-a34e-5097-bfd1-c65783c31fe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453452} {:answer/id #uuid "0e0e000c-633e-51cc-bcaf-35de257a2bd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453453}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453452} :answer-field/id #uuid "feaa10ce-c2f4-5d9a-998c-4aa5896d0d74" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7e4c0564-b5b1-55bc-bc17-3dffa3a951a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-158601" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y<-1},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=-1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(1,-1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- **II.** The point $P(1,-1)$ belongs to $A$\n- **III.** The point $Q(2,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie below the line $y=-1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/75/75d5d199d12cc2f3170822a40c52762fcc8368caac26a8134661f9ac72ab462b.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,-1)$ and $Q(2,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/81/818da293be040897d3a536b396d6fe7ff992f3d91f976301e14a6253356b5bde.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1,-1)$ satisfy $y=-1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(-1,1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y<-1.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"} :step/id #uuid "b1e2a497-e042-518c-bac4-15b77f002a69" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417820}}] :activity/title "Interior and Boundary Points" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417784}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481682} :activity/id #uuid "2e2eeeff-af14-5c58-b935-c783ef93c698" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048942 :topic/id #uuid "cdb710e4-10cf-5554-8041-f24c448bbdae" :topic/math-academy-id 1930} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481682 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "435fbf66-881a-599f-a5de-59147174d9f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477860} {:answer/id #uuid "469b4926-a18a-5a39-87cf-c8473bed4b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477872} {:answer/id #uuid "89babb92-9de9-5f2f-b11d-064346a1f1ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478013} {:answer/id #uuid "99e2c243-585a-5ab6-a643-ace8c4066e27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478033} {:answer/id #uuid "abf4ed0d-fc7b-5e79-816f-b20987508d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478064}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477872} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eb96924-1d1d-506d-a661-5befdb8dbe4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e5330869-80f9-574d-be15-bcaa4f259731" :question/math-academy-id "q-36429" :question/problem "The set $B$ and its boundary $∂B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}<1\\},\\,∂B=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The point $P(\\sqrt{2},1)$ lies on the boundary of $B$\n\nII. The point $P(\\sqrt{2},1)$ belongs to $B$\n\nIII. The point $Q(0,-1)$ is an interior point of $B$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n\\{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $B$ consists of all points that lie inside the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/97/97ded2e99adb83b69bc547cef822c46ac2134d3ed17c9cdf65f24083560afae5.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(\\sqrt{2},1)$ and $Q(0,-1)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/38/38a11d6712843367b08ff98843b3c3daf43729d82adbe70e15b8019fb68cee6c.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(\\sqrt{2},1)$ satisfy the equation $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂B,$ the boundary of $B.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(\\sqrt{2},1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}<1.$ Hence, $P$ does not belongs to $B.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is fully contained inside the set $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and III only'."} :step/id #uuid "1c6c6de6-cfd8-5a68-9102-8e31ed8dc6af" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481723}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481687 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376082 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9d5b57d-d397-5e8b-9a46-ca703dc128d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453430} {:answer/id #uuid "654fdd13-26b0-5b13-a201-e47a6bbd127f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453431}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453431} :answer-field/id #uuid "f245c56b-ea24-5bc6-9714-193572353072" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454465} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c17641c7-2616-5f44-913a-204e61d02795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453432} {:answer/id #uuid "1798276f-6c7e-5444-91e4-9f13f58ce650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453433}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453433} :answer-field/id #uuid "2595354c-4c90-5eb8-bc56-8aa27847f7cd" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454466} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f28fa17-78af-5e9c-9940-0619d6581c75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453434} {:answer/id #uuid "03c6eb37-ee8b-5456-b1b0-d792a68992e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453435}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453434} :answer-field/id #uuid "55044069-05bd-53df-bf38-5763c4c860a7" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "80400990-b978-5e6c-95c6-54cde07862e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-158534" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y\\le x+2},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=x+2}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $P(2,4)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- **II.** The point $P(2,4)$ belongs to $A$\n- **III.** The point $Q(-2,3)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or below the line $y=x+2.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/3b/3b0505f0c1fb295665dd64c9d0f26f23960b638ef0e1d414c7ad306a89154dfb.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(2,4)$ and $Q(-2,3)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/62/623f00c423c7a846c52537d58c071c730083ba963fb488dbedbb76637ea9cc8c.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(2,4)$ satisfy the equation $y=x+2.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(2,4)$ satisfy the inequality $y\\le x+4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'"} :step/id #uuid "310f06ad-505e-54b4-996b-7dc30a68d2ee" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481705}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481701 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d436c48-b5c4-585f-a38c-5dc69c2efe0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464636} {:answer/id #uuid "c71daeb2-9b0f-5776-bfeb-ca2ae15dac67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464637}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464637} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8cc30b0-4919-5870-8b93-d9d25272b3a6" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465252} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b87f9f55-43d7-553b-9dfe-3a193c6e8fe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464638} {:answer/id #uuid "def2f8dd-7369-5c86-a313-88ab5e541789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464639}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464639} :answer-field/id #uuid "34182fca-1665-5d7a-8612-d5439b2e05ab" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465253} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee0abd33-f10b-55b9-a007-2ea3f5540421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464640} {:answer/id #uuid "1e6f1c65-b9cc-55d0-abbc-f7595924d4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464641}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464641} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d9d88a2-2fcb-55a4-b7cf-4375c94b9208" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9430b4bf-f1ad-5467-9486-5b90e2e64bb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-159550" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e999290971eed02d7f12f9154bdb4d04f3328fd250b385059d94e83e3f195b2.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/f1/f10ff87f64dc07adb8e9535926186341bab88f2d0c783475ef8fd57b06b35fc0.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $B$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III'."} :step/id #uuid "5defe44d-af9c-50de-9c3a-215ee05792f2"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481705 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eb02c31-7aaa-5f27-b88d-0bac5e5ad915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471095} {:answer/id #uuid "b97e2bd8-7962-5731-ba0e-3fbbb462b382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471096}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186471096} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d50bd4b-9d59-580e-80c2-44d2596ae2d2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472053} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2061a619-a5e4-54b1-91b8-46d58be4ebda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471097} {:answer/id #uuid "0fe9756c-7be4-5f9a-b46e-b99e86db76bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471098}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186471097} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a8ac79c-036b-59a8-86e0-7503a1be86e8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472054} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c8bab23-b03e-52ee-ae9d-30f1b5066d53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471099} {:answer/id #uuid "5d0e0cf0-0b66-548e-8160-cac53743805a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471100}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186471100} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cffa612-f7f9-54e4-b510-6f7c9d3f046b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "82499ed8-8929-5ffd-bdf0-732a59ce454e" :question/math-academy-id "q-159265" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4a50ae5f62dc5c52d0060a969d510453d3f922fad8f7ba887b2b1ee77fceae6.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/e8/e847aa005e917a88b19566933e3770edd05ddee946fc25dfd6fed91016bb909d.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/51/51439f6021efd768268e59c63627449cc68c9a420c4c7a80ab6773dd46e6ec1f.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/dd/ddd471167f604b4ba97b36ddff3af6be7c7d3cfad6021a4cfc779c6f8ebb2a3d.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and III only.'"} :step/id #uuid "64801b9b-8548-5aee-9429-d836c284e4aa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481701}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481723 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06ad12bb-2aee-5f41-ab40-c7e07a29ecc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459865} {:answer/id #uuid "01c3dbc7-f7af-57f0-b1fa-a62993667554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459866}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459866} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8d9e536-a559-5926-85d7-959a922313a8" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460799} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2326431-abad-5546-8bd6-d752a2d42a78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459867} {:answer/id #uuid "95aa5abb-9709-5cff-931e-02fc205a698b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459868}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459868} :answer-field/id #uuid "7570d9d3-0c06-5e02-b6ac-3f8b39e3de82" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460800} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44150225-85ba-53e8-894f-8dc3b204e3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459869} {:answer/id #uuid "c060cc95-7b2b-536a-80dc-89828039a2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459870}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459869} :answer-field/id #uuid "bac9c0b8-6db5-5d97-8aab-3bb2c71951f1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "560c5161-e608-551f-9c8f-70f39e328770" :question/math-academy-id "q-36432" :question/problem "![](images/a5/a50bf96047d64846b704a54ce965c9cf2c8c7916810b26849aacffe3ace84e60.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/90/9072fe06b5821810a152f992cdd3d852ccb4cd0744c3a63119ba2634d3e3121d.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ The point $C$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only'."} :step/id #uuid "9b477457-6b55-5d87-a037-106ef4b7ead7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481687}}] :activity/title "Interior and Boundary Points" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481566}]}
